\subsection{Noise-Free Model-Mismatch Floor}
\label{sec:model-mismatch-ablation}

A separate ablation removes simulated sensor corruption while leaving each
estimator otherwise in its deployed configuration.  The eight stationary
JONSWAP and PM--Stokes records are replayed through OU--II, OU--III, and TFG
with the simulator's \texttt{--no-noise} option.  Accelerometer and gyroscope
white noise, initial bias, bias random walk, and magnetometer noise and
calibration perturbations are therefore absent, while the physical wave and
attitude motion are unchanged.  The estimators are \emph{not} retuned for ideal
measurements: their process/measurement covariances, pseudo-measurements,
adaptation, startup logic, and regularization remain those used in normal
operation.  The experiment consequently measures a deployed
estimator/model-mismatch floor, not pure plant-model mismatch.

All values in Table~\ref{tab:model-mismatch-floor} pool the final \SI{900}{s}
of the eight equal-length records.  With identical window lengths, the pooled
RMS is the RMS obtained by concatenating the eight scored windows.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Noise-free deployed-estimator mismatch floor over eight stationary
  records.  $Z/Z_{\rm ref}$ is normalized by pooled reference vertical RMS.}
  \label{tab:model-mismatch-floor}
  \footnotesize
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    Family & $X$ [m] & $Y$ [m] & $Z$ [m] & 3-D [m] & $Z/Z_{\rm ref}$ [\%] \\
    \midrule
    OU--II & 0.326 & 0.203 & 0.258 & 0.462 & 21.20 \\
    OU--III & 0.247 & 0.160 & 0.144 & 0.328 & 11.88 \\
    TFG & 0.234 & 0.152 & 0.146 & 0.315 & 11.98 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

OU--III's pooled vertical and 3-D displacement RMS are
\SI{0.144}{m} and \SI{0.328}{m}; OU--II gives
\SI{0.258}{m} and \SI{0.462}{m}, and TFG gives
\SI{0.146}{m} and \SI{0.315}{m}.
The error remains nonzero with ideal sensors. Figure~\ref{fig:model-mismatch-floor}
shows displacement, attitude and the bias states created by each estimator.
The underlying motion is the estimated sailboat CG response; normalization
by $H_s$ uses incident wave height, not measured vessel heave height.

\IfFileExists{ou_model_mismatch_floor.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_model_mismatch_floor}
    \caption{Pooled noise-free mismatch over eight vessel-response records. The gyro-bias panel uses a logarithmic scale. True sensor biases are zero, so nonzero bias states are estimator-generated errors.}
    \label{fig:model-mismatch-floor}
  \end{figure}
}{}

\IfFileExists{ou_model_mismatch_scaling.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_model_mismatch_scaling}
    \caption{Noise-free residual against incident significant wave height. Solid lines are JONSWAP and dashed lines are PM. The dotted slope-one guide is a reference, not a fitted scaling law; the middle panel divides by incident $H_s$.}
    \label{fig:model-mismatch-scaling}
  \end{figure}
}{}

Fig.~\ref{fig:model-mismatch-scaling} separates the per-record behavior.
The observed vertical-error ranges, normalized by incident $H_s$, are
\pct{5.39}--\pct{9.24} for OU--II,
\pct{2.94}--\pct{3.15} for OU--III,
\pct{2.96}--\pct{3.18} for TFG.
These are observed ranges over the prescribed ensemble, not uniform error bounds.

The filters retain their deployed process and measurement covariance,
pseudo-measurements, adaptation and startup logic. This ablation therefore
measures their combined deterministic residual under ideal measurements; it
does not isolate one stochastic prior, one regularizer or a hull-model error.
